**Contract-version chicken-and-egg**: B1's own precedent is that `lowering-contract-version` lives in src as the canonical source, and B0's test imports and compares it. R0's frozen file predates this file and could not import a not-yet-built src namespace, so it hardcoded its own copy instead, and its file box forbids editing it after the fact. I applied B1's precedent one-sidedly: `debruijn-register-code/contract-version` (=1) is the canonical source; my own test file requires R0's test namespace and asserts `r0/register-contract-version = rcode/contract-version`, the cross-check R0 couldn't add for itself.

**Register image shape**: not a flat vector like the stack image — `{:bodies [{:locals :registers :start :end} ...], :instructions [...]}`, matching design section 3's "body ranges plus positional instructions." Every operand (including bare register indices and jump/body-pc targets) is encoded via `debruijn-code/encode-scalar` as a `:long`/`:vector`/`:keyword` scalar — zero reimplemented hex/framing code; the whole encoder is reused, not forked.

**Validator rules and their tests**: `nonempty`/`mnemonic-rule`/`arity-rule`/`operand-kind-rule`/`target-bounds-rule` mirror B1's structural rules over the new opcode table (`validator-accepts-every-corpus-image`, `validator-accepts-b2-fixtures`). `body-scope-rule` (bodies contiguously partition the instruction vector; every `:closure` body-pc names a real body of matching arity) and `register-bounds-rule` (every `:reg`/`:regs` operand within its own body's declared register count) are new to this format, with no B1 equivalent, since a stack image has no register file to bound against (`validator-rejects-out-of-range-register-reference`). `terminator-rule` checks every body ends `:return`, body 0 `:halt` (`validator-rejects-malformed-terminator`). `validator-rejects-out-of-range-jump-target` covers `target-bounds-rule`.

**Allocator determinism** (design 4.3), each rule's enforcement site: virtual-value definition order = evaluation order (`lower-node!`'s own recursive walk, unconditional); register selection = lowest-available (`allocate-temp!`'s `(first free)` over a `sorted-set`); tie-break = lowest virtual-id (temp indices assigned strictly increasing when no free slot exists); no host map/set iteration order affects output (`sorted-set` for free-list, no unordered map ever walked to produce bytes). No `:move` is ever emitted — the lowerer is target-register-passing (caller supplies the destination register directly), so no relocation step exists; this is asserted implicitly by every corpus program's validator pass and explicitly noted in both files' docstrings, not separately unit-tested by mnemonic-absence (a gap I did not add a dedicated test for). All of this is pinned by `lowering-is-deterministic-across-repeated-runs` (image AND R equal across two independent lowerings, whole register-lowerable corpus).

**Three-way address law**: `register-image-var-addresses` reads `:load-bound`/`:load-free` operands off `:instructions` in pc order — no separate walk needed, since the register image's own instruction order already equals the lowering walk order (main body first, then out-of-line bodies in discovery order, occurrences expanded positionally, by construction of `lower-node!`/`lower-register`). Extended R0's own two-way corpus (`address-law-holds-three-way-over-the-b0-parity-corpus`, `...-over-b2s-extra-fixtures`) rather than duplicating R0's fixture-building code, deref'ing R0's private vars/fns (`#'r0/resolved-addresses-of`, `#'r0/stack-addresses-of`, `@#'r0/address-law-extra-fixtures`) instead of copying them. Excludes the three R2-scoped corpus programs (`ast-has-deferred-type?` filter), since the resolved/stack sides support them but the register side correctly refuses them.

**Lift law** — this was the hard part. I initially designed a top-down recursive-descent parser and hit a real bug: an atomic instruction's own shape can't tell you whether it's a complete expression or just the first of a chain (e.g. an application's operator, itself atomic, isn't the whole application). I rebuilt the lift as a single left-to-right **postfix-stack parser** (`parse-range`): the register pc stream, read in order, is reverse-Polish over an implicit value stack — `:call` pops `argc+1` items and pushes one combined value, `:branch-false`/`:jump` fork into recursively-parsed consequent/alternate sub-ranges (their exact pc bounds read directly off the branch-false/jump operands, no guessing). The second bug, found the same way (differential-tested against `named-canonical-vector ∘ lower`, not just the validator): `[:push]` in the target format is only emitted once a stack value's SIBLING is about to be computed, or right before `:call` consumes the topmost operand — never for a value consumed as an `if`'s test, never for a body's unconsumed final return value. My first version batched all pushes at `:call` time, which is byte-identical for a flat application but silently wrong once an operand is itself an `:if` with something before it (`(+ (if t 1 2) 3)` class of program) — the earlier sibling's push has to land before the nested if's own tuples, not after. Fixed with `finalize-top`, applied immediately before stacking a new sibling and immediately before `:call` consumes its popped operands.

Tested for exactly the 4 required programs (`lift-with-the-lowerers-own-side-table-is-exact`, `lift-with-synthesized-names-is-alpha-equivalent`): simple (`worked-example`, includes a lambda+application+nested-call), duplicate-parameter (`(fn [x x] x)`), free-variable, and nested/shared-occurrence (`nested-closure`, two levels, duplicate binder at the outer level) — plus the two B2 shared-occurrence fixtures (`shared-variable-under-two-contexts`, `shared-lambda-under-two-contexts`) and a standalone `if` and nested-if-inside-a-call case during interactive verification (not left as a committed test beyond what's listed, since the completion criteria only names the 4). Exact-side-table equality checked against `named-canonical-vector ∘ yin.vm.linearize/lower` (the true oracle, independent of B2's own lowerer), not merely against B2's stack image.

